@prefix actuation: <http://example.org/orexis/actuation#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/actuation#> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/actuation> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis actuation" ;
    rdfs:comment """Touching the physical world. Held by the RESOURCE OWNER, not by the agent that won — a claim is a claim on the owner, redeemed by it. Dosing is a fact about the hardware and is stated on the device, so recalibrating is a genesis edit rather than a code change.""" .

:Actuation a ag:Capability ; rdfs:label "Actuation" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent may drive actuators it owns, bounded by a validated claim and by the device's own cap. No plant agent has this: winning water is not the same as being able to open a valve." .

#  `actuation:Actuator` was here, and it was `sosa:Actuator` wearing our name.
#
#  It was an intersection, not a synonym — `ag:Device ∧ sosa:Actuator` — and that is why the earlier
#  audits kept it. But a named class is not the only way to state an intersection: an instance
#  can carry both types, and the two constraints can sit side by side in the one shape that
#  enforced the conjunction. What the name bought was saying it once; what it cost was a reader
#  having to learn that our word for a valve is not the standard one.
#
#  `ag:Device` STAYS, and why is the principle this whole audit turns on.
#
#  SOSA's axis is FUNCTIONAL: `sosa:Platform` asks *where is it mounted* — its own examples are
#  a post, a buoy, a ship, a cell-phone, a human — and `ssn:System` asks *what implements the
#  procedure*. Neither is about what a thing is made of, and the spec is explicit that all its
#  major classes may be virtual, a correction it made deliberately.
#
#  `ag:Device`'s axis is SUBSTRATE: a physical edge node with legs, interchangeable with a
#  stand-in. That is a different question, so a term answering it necessarily cuts ACROSS their
#  classes rather than duplicating one — `mc:Microcontroller` and `mc:Peripheral` are Platforms,
#  sensors and actuators are Systems, and `mc:hasPin`, `mc:logicVolts` and `ag:simulatedBy` all
#  take `ag:Device` as their domain. Boards have pins.
#
#  So the test for keeping one of ours is not whether a standard has a similar word. It is
#  whether the standard is answering the same QUESTION. Here it is not, which is why
#  `actuation:Actuator` goes and `ag:Device` stays.
#
#  See knowledge/decisions/one-word-for-one-relation.md.

:Valve a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Device , sosa:Actuator ; rdfs:label "Valve" ;
    rdfs:comment "Opens for a bounded time to deliver to one subject. Verifies its command before acting and runs its own fail-safe." .

:hasActuator a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range sosa:Actuator ;
    rdfs:label "has actuator" .
:actuates a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain sosa:Actuator ;
    rdfs:label "actuates" ;
    rdfs:comment "The subject this device delivers to — the map from a won claim to real hardware." .
:drawsFrom a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain sosa:Actuator ;
    rdfs:label "draws from" .

:mlPerSecond a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain sosa:Actuator ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "ml per second" ;
    rdfs:comment "Flow calibration: converts an amount won into device-open seconds." .
:maxDoseMl a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain sosa:Actuator ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "max dose ml" ;
    rdfs:comment "Hard cap on a single dose — defence in depth, independent of whatever cleared." .

#################  What the agent believes about waiting  #################
#  A dose is commanded and then CONFIRMED, and the two are separated by however long the pour
#  takes plus however long the bus needs. The agent already knows the first — it computed the
#  open-seconds itself, from the device's own calibration — so the only thing it has to state
#  is the second, and stating it as slack rather than as an absolute is what keeps a slow valve
#  and a silent one apart.
#
#  The same shape as `sensing:readingGraceS`, and for the same reason: there too the agent
#  chooses the interval and can only be relative to it. An absolute deadline would contradict
#  the agent's own instruction — it cannot both ask a valve for a ninety-second pour and call
#  the valve late after thirty.

:doseGraceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "dose grace seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long after a dose SHOULD have finished this agent still waits for the device to say it did. Added to the open-seconds the agent computed for that dose, never used alone: the deadline is this dose's own duration plus this slack. Silence past it is not proof the water did not flow — the report may simply be lost — which is why it is reported and counted rather than acted on. See knowledge/decisions/an-unconfirmed-dose-is-not-a-delivered-one.md." .

#################  Who takes a dose  #################

#  The link from the Actuate row this package ships to the code that pours — a family of one,
#  and `agent.providers` returns it by the same road it returns sensing's two. See
#  knowledge/domain/actor.md.
ag:Actuate ag:takenBy :Actuation .
